{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018aaqJbbgHUiHsHVERNsUPY","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on the context filesystem, the discussion was about whether to reuse the existing metadata record type (API key 1 - UnregisterBrokerRecord) or introduce a new record type for unregistering controllers.\\\\n\\\\n**The decision was to introduce a NEW record type** rather than reuse the existing metadata record.\\\\n\\\\nThe key reasoning is documented in `/store/kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-13-mail-bf2eb97f1e7622fe0746cec3-69f36f36.md`:\\\\n\\\\n> \\\\\\\"Without a separate unregistration record, it is difficult to determine during replay which entity is being unregistered.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nThis is particularly important because of the combined mode scenario where a broker and controller can share the same node ID. As Kevin Wu explained in the email:\\\\n\\\\n> \\\\\\\"When the controller replays this record, should it unregister the broker or the controller? The answer is not super obvious. I think the only way to distinguish between the broker and controller un-registrations is by looking if the `brokerEpoch` is set in the record, but that seems less intuitive than introducing a separate record.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nJosé Armando García Sancio (in `/store/kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-12-mail-f884fe02638d7de53fef380a-24f3c9a1.md`) had initially suggested reusing the ApiKey 1 record by renaming it to UnregisterNodeRecord, but after offline discussion, they decided that the ambiguity in combined mode justified introducing a new unregistration controller record instead.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-12-mail-f884fe02638d7de53fef380a-24f3c9a1.md\\\",\\\"kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-13-mail-bf2eb97f1e7622fe0746cec3-69f36f36.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":21.11,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":1,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":73148,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":13972,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.042818800000000004,\\\"input_tokens\\\":50,\\\"output_tokens\\\":1502}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2ab3379-b4a5-4003-9ee2-afc9f5b67e42","uuid":"8e67bf26-f051-430b-8257-751b34ae742d","timestamp":"2026-09-27T19:19:39.735Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on the context filesystem, the discussion was about whether to reuse the existing metadata record type (API key 1 - UnregisterBrokerRecord) or introduce a new record type for unregistering controllers.\\\\n\\\\n**The decision was to introduce a NEW record type** rather than reuse the existing metadata record.\\\\n\\\\nThe key reasoning is documented in `/store/kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-13-mail-bf2eb97f1e7622fe0746cec3-69f36f36.md`:\\\\n\\\\n> \\\\\\\"Without a separate unregistration record, it is difficult to determine during replay which entity is being unregistered.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nThis is particularly important because of the combined mode scenario where a broker and controller can share the same node ID. As Kevin Wu explained in the email:\\\\n\\\\n> \\\\\\\"When the controller replays this record, should it unregister the broker or the controller? The answer is not super obvious. I think the only way to distinguish between the broker and controller un-registrations is by looking if the `brokerEpoch` is set in the record, but that seems less intuitive than introducing a separate record.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nJosé Armando García Sancio (in `/store/kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-12-mail-f884fe02638d7de53fef380a-24f3c9a1.md`) had initially suggested reusing the ApiKey 1 record by renaming it to UnregisterNodeRecord, but after offline discussion, they decided that the ambiguity in combined mode justified introducing a new unregistration controller record instead.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-12-mail-f884fe02638d7de53fef380a-24f3c9a1.md\\\",\\\"kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-13-mail-bf2eb97f1e7622fe0746cec3-69f36f36.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":21.11,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":1,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":73148,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":13972,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.042818800000000004,\\\"input_tokens\\\":50,\\\"output_tokens\\\":1502}}\"}","_meta":{"fastmcp":{"wrap_result":true}},"structuredContent":{"result":"{\"answer\":\"Based on the context filesystem, the discussion was about whether to reuse the existing metadata record type (API key 1 - UnregisterBrokerRecord) or introduce a new record type for unregistering controllers.\\n\\n**The decision was to introduce a NEW record type** rather than reuse the existing metadata record.\\n\\nThe key reasoning is documented in `/store/kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-13-mail-bf2eb97f1e7622fe0746cec3-69f36f36.md`:\\n\\n> \\\"Without a separate unregistration record, it is difficult to determine during replay which entity is being unregistered.\\\"\\n\\nThis is particularly important because of the combined mode scenario where a broker and controller can share the same node ID. As Kevin Wu explained in the email:\\n\\n> \\\"When the controller replays this record, should it unregister the broker or the controller? The answer is not super obvious. I think the only way to distinguish between the broker and controller un-registrations is by looking if the `brokerEpoch` is set in the record, but that seems less intuitive than introducing a separate record.\\\"\\n\\nJosé Armando García Sancio (in `/store/kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-12-mail-f884fe02638d7de53fef380a-24f3c9a1.md`) had initially suggested reusing the ApiKey 1 record by renaming it to UnregisterNodeRecord, but after offline discussion, they decided that the ambiguity in combined mode justified introducing a new unregistration controller record instead.\",\"error\":null,\"files\":[\"kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-12-mail-f884fe02638d7de53fef380a-24f3c9a1.md\",\"kips/KIP-1312/2026-05-13-mail-bf2eb97f1e7622fe0746cec3-69f36f36.md\"],\"seconds\":21.11,\"truncated\":false,\"turns\":1,\"usage\":{\"cache_read_tokens\":73148,\"cache_write